===Determiner===
{{head|en|determiner|first-person plural possessive determiner|cat2=possessive determiners}}

# Belonging to [[us]], excluding the person(s) being addressed {{q|exclusive ''our''}}.
#: {{ux|en|Sorry, you're not invited to '''our''' party.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2008|author=Mike Knudson; Steve Wilkinson|title=Raymond and Graham Rule the School
|passage=Paying no attention to Lizzy, Mrs. Gibson began calling out '''our''' names in alphabetical order.}}
# Belonging to [[us]], including the person(s) being addressed {{q|inclusive ''our''}}.
#: {{ux|en|We must try to save '''our''' marriage for the sake of '''our''' children.}}
# Of, from, or belonging to any entity that the speaker is a part of or identifies with, such as place of employment or education, [[nation]], [[region]], [[language]], etc.
#: {{ux|en|'''Our''' safety record at ABC Widgets is very good.}}
#: {{ux|en|In English, '''our''' nouns do not generally have gender.}}
#* {{RQ:Schuster Hepaticae|volume=V|page=viii|text=Thirdly, I continue to attempt to interdigitate the taxa in '''our''' flora with taxa of the remainder of the world.}}
# Belonging to people in general.
#: {{ux|en|As we get older, '''our''' reactions get slower.}}
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|year=2013|month=July-August|author=[http://www.americanscientist.org/authors/detail/stephen-p-lownie Stephen P. Lownie]; [http://www.americanscientist.org/authors/detail/david-m-pelz David M. Pelz]|magazine=w:American Scientist|title=Stents to Prevent Stroke|url=http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2013/4/stents-to-prevent-stroke|passage=As we age, the major arteries of '''our''' bodies frequently become thickened with plaque, a fatty material with an oatmeal-like consistency that builds up along the inner lining of blood vessels.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20170529010543/http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2013/4/stents-to-prevent-stroke|archivedate=29 May 2017}}
# {{lb|en|colloquial}} Belonging to everyone being addressed.
#: {{ux|en|How are we all tonight? Have we got '''our''' happy faces on?}}
# {{lb|en|colloquial|may seem patronising}} Belonging to an individual being addressed; {{n-g|used especially of a person in the speaker's care, or to whom advice or instruction is being given.}}
#: {{ux|en|How are we feeling this morning, Mrs Miggins? Have we taken '''our''' tablets yet?}}
#: {{ux|en|You've been spending all your money boozing when you have a wife and family to support. I think we need to get '''our''' priorities straight.}}
# {{lb|en|colloquial|uncommon}} {{n-g|Belonging to a third person, especially someone in the speaker's care.}} 
#: {{ux|en|"I've just been to see Mrs Miggins." — "And how is she?" — "Not too good today. We've had a little problem with '''our''' tummy."}}
# {{lb|en|colloquial}} {{n-g|Used to imply connection between the speaker's experiences or activities and a group of listeners}}.
#: {{ux|en|Hey guys, in this video I'll show you how I make my upside-down sponge cake. First, let's get '''our''' ingredients weighed out.}}
# {{lb|en|Northern England|Scotland}} {{n-g|Used before a person's name to indicate that the person is in one's family, or is a very close friend.}}
#: {{ux|en|I'm going to see '''our''' Terry for tea.}}

